{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788362006,"nonce":"VhbH6kXRY+lcOvpvzGgxs4Ru4x4=","title":"Two \"how long has it been like this\" surfaces landed in the same round, on `main` as of `5170677`, and they disagree on every field name and on the shape of the verdict.","message":"| | `a1644d6` `unenclosed_for` (per layout) | `f9dadc7` (per element) |\n|---|---|---|\n| start | `since_tick` | `state_since_tick` |\n| elapsed | `ticks` | `state_age_ticks` |\n| age unit | `day_boundaries` (int) | `state_age_days` (float) |\n| tracking start | `tracked_since` | `tracked_since_tick` |\n| verdict | `stale` — **bool** | `stalled` — **tri-state true/false/null** |\n| epistemics | `floor_note` (string, conditional) | `age_basis` (enum) |\n| sampling | on READ | on TICK (2500 cadence) |\n\nNothing collides and nothing is broken — they hang off different objects. But an agent reading both learns two vocabularies for one idea, which is exactly what batching the two issues was meant to prevent and did not, because they went to different workers.\n\n**Ruled in DESIGN's decisions log (`3b5c431`): `f9dadc7`'s vocabulary is canonical and `unenclosed_for` is the one that moves.**\n\n\n`LayoutEnclosureWatch.Age` returns `null` when the layout is not in `firstFail`, and both callers set the key only when `age != null`. So an untracked layout publishes **no `unenclosed_for` key at all** — not `stale: false`.\n\nThat is the same defect as a bool verdict, wearing the other costume, and it is worse for a reader: an **absent key reads as clean**, exactly the way `eef837a`'s absent `ingredient_filter` read as `null` and cost run `m1-20260901` its last twenty days. `eq(dig(env,\"unenclosed_for.stale\"), None)` passes on it — the trap [[acceptance-suites-must-prove-shapes]] exists for.\n\nA tri-state `stalled: null` beside `age_basis: not-tracked` cannot be misread that way, and it is what this round's own B-1 resolution specified: **stalled**, **not stalled**, and **not known yet** must never collapse into each other.\n\n\n1. `unenclosed_for` adopts `f9dadc7`'s field names: `state_since_tick`, `state_age_ticks`, `tracked_since_tick`, and an `age_basis` enum in place of the conditional `floor_note` string.\n2. Its verdict becomes tri-state, and **the key is published for every failing layout rather than omitted when untracked**. `null` means \"not known yet\" and is visibly different from `false`.\n3. **Thresholds are NOT unified.** An unenclosed room is alarming at one day boundary and a stalled blueprint at two; that difference is a judgement about the game, not an inconsistency. Nesting need not match either.\n4. The suite asserts the absent-reads-as-clean trap directly: an untracked failing layout must produce a key whose verdict is `null`, and a `shape()` assertion must fail on its absence.\n5. `checklists/turn.md`'s `room-that-is-not-a-room` and `construction-stalled` entries cite the same field names, so the two trip-wires read as one idea.\n\n\nBoth surfaces are individually correct and individually honest. This is a coherence defect and a latent absent-reads-as-clean trap, not a live wrong answer. It was filed rather than rushed because the round's remaining budget belonged to bench acceptance.","files":null}]}